add(46,'reshit-chokhmah-except-leave',4,'Reshit Chokhmah, Gate of Holiness 17:26',sub(he('Reshit Chokhmah, Gate of Holiness 17',26),'הכלל שאין לך דבר'),
'The rule is that there is nothing that cannot be repaired through repentance. When the Zohar explains that it does not depend on repentance, this means that its repentance is difficult. Being shown that it does not depend on repentance is itself part of that difficulty. Nothing stands in the way of repentance. Even if you hear, “Return, wayward children, except so-and-so,” as in the case of Elisha Acher, pay no heed. After all, Elisha Acher was ultimately accepted even though he did not repent; all the more so, had he repented, it would have helped him still more. Rather, they closed the doors of repentance before him, and he needed to persist until the entrance opened. This is what the Sages meant: “Do whatever the householder tells you, except ‘Leave.’” The householder is the Holy One, blessed be He. Whatever He tells you to do of the mitzvot, do—except “Leave.” If He says to you, “Leave My house and do not enter,” as in Elisha Acher’s case, do not listen to Him; instead, enter through repentance. For this is the householder’s desire, but He is misleading you.',
'This is the original interpretation behind “except ‘Leave.’” The apparent rejection is itself a test of persistence: the passage expressly distinguishes difficult repentance from impossible repentance.',pg([129,130],[175,176]))
add(46,'tanya-knesset-yisrael-source-of-souls',13,'Tanya, Part I; Likkutei Amarim 52:3-4',he('Tanya, Part I; Likkutei Amarim 52',3)+' '+he('Tanya, Part I; Likkutei Amarim 52',4),
'This source is called “the revealed world,” “the Matron,” “the lower Mother,” and “Shekhinah,” from the expression “I shall dwell among them.” It is so called because this source is the beginning of the revelation of the light of the Infinite, which it draws down and illuminates into the worlds in a revealed manner. From this source, the particular light and vitality appropriate to each one is drawn forth, dwelling and clothing itself within them to give them life. Therefore it is metaphorically called “the mother of the children” and “Knesset Yisrael”: from this source the souls of Atzilut were emanated, the souls of Beriah were created, and so forth. All of them come only from the extension of vitality and light from this source called Shekhinah, like the extension of light from the sun.',
'Knesset Yisrael here names the shared spiritual source of Israel’s souls. This explains why the ois can speak of Israel collectively through a single feminine figure.',pg([131,132],[177]),anchorEnd='כְּנֶסֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל,')
# Local-source quotation inserted below after canonical books have been restored.
add(46,'radak-woman-seeking-her-husband',17,'Radak on Jeremiah 31:22:3',he('Radak on Jeremiah 31:22',3),
'“For Hashem has created a new thing in the land”: He will create a new thing after you have remained in exile for many days. What is the new thing? “A woman shall go about a man.” Ordinarily, the man seeks and goes about after the woman, as the Sages likewise said: The owner of a lost object searches for his lost object. Then the woman will go about after her husband. That is, the children of Israel will return to Hashem their God, and He will redeem them. Likewise, in Hoshea’s prophecy: “Afterward the children of Israel shall return and seek Hashem their God and David their king, and they shall come trembling to Hashem and to His goodness in the latter days.”',
'Radak identifies the woman’s initiative with Israel returning to God. His explanation supplies the prophetic meaning of the reversal of the ordinary pattern of seeking.',pg([132,133],[178]))
add(47,'kohelet-rabbah-saul-compassion',4,'Kohelet Rabbah 7:16:1',he('Kohelet Rabbah 7:16',1),
'“Do not be excessively righteous, and do not be overly wise”: Do not be more righteous than your Creator. This speaks of Shaul, as it is written, “Shaul came to the city of Amalek.” Rabbi Huna and Rabbi Benaya say: He began arguing against his Creator, saying: The Holy One, blessed be He, said, “Go and strike Amalek.” If the men sinned, what did the women sin? What did the children sin? What did the cattle, ox, and donkey sin? A heavenly voice went forth and said, “Do not be excessively righteous”—more than your Creator. The Sages say: He began arguing from the heifer whose neck is broken, saying: Scripture says, “They shall break the heifer’s neck there in the wadi.” He kills, yet she has her neck broken! If a human being sinned, what did the animal sin? A heavenly voice went forth and said, “Do not be excessively righteous.” Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish says: Whoever becomes compassionate where severity is called for will ultimately become cruel where compassion is called for. From where do we know that he became cruel where compassion was called for? It is stated, “He struck Nob, the city of the priests, by the sword.” Should Nob not have been treated at least like the offspring of Amalek? The Sages say: Whoever becomes compassionate where severity is called for will ultimately be struck by the attribute of judgment, as it is stated, “Shaul and his three sons died.”',
'The Midrash contrasts compassion governed by the divine command with a personal impulse that can turn into cruelty elsewhere. Rav Tzadok uses this contrast to explain why even an attractive character trait needs to be directed toward God.',pg([133,134],[178,179]))
add(47,'ritva-heavenly-matters-and-forgiveness',9,'Ritva on Megillah 28a:4',he('Ritva on Megillah 28a',4),
'“Nor did my fellow’s curse come upon my bed”—as with Mar Zutra son of Rav Nachman, who, when going up to his bed, would say: May everyone who has distressed me be released and forgiven. As for the statement that any Torah scholar who does not take vengeance and bear a grudge like a snake is no Torah scholar: that concerns someone who sinned against him in matters of Heaven, and disciplining those who sin.',
'Ritva distinguishes forgiveness of personal injury from firmness in matters of Heaven. This is the qualification needed for the ois’s contrast between natural anger and anger directed toward a sacred purpose.',pg([134,135],[180]))
add(47,'bamidbar-rabbah-prophets-compassion',13,'Bamidbar Rabbah 20:1',sub(he('Bamidbar Rabbah 20:1'),'ראה מה בין'),
'See the difference between the prophets of Israel and the prophets of the idolaters. The prophets of Israel warn Israel against transgressions, as it is stated, “You, son of man, I have appointed you as a watchman,” and so forth. The prophet who arose from the nations made a breach through which to destroy creatures from the world. Moreover, all the prophets showed compassion toward Israel and toward the idolaters. Thus Yirmeyahu says, “My heart moans for Moab like flutes,” and likewise Yechezkel: “Son of man, raise a lament over Tyre.” But this cruel one arose to uproot an entire nation for nothing, without cause. Therefore the account of Bilam was written, to make known why the Holy One, blessed be He, removed the holy spirit from the idolaters: this one arose from among them, and see what he did.',
'The Midrash’s evidence includes Israel’s prophets mourning for Moab and Tyre. It therefore makes their compassion broader than concern for their own people, and contrasts this with Bilam’s destructive purpose.',pg([135,136],[181]))
add(47,'avot-action-and-world-to-come',18,'Pirkei Avot 4:17',he('Pirkei Avot 4:17'),
'He used to say: One hour of repentance and good deeds in this world is better than all the life of the World to Come; and one hour of contentment in the World to Come is better than all the life of this world.',
'The Mishnah preserves both sides of the comparison: this world offers the distinctive opportunity for repentance and action, while the World to Come offers a kind of contentment beyond earthly life.',pg([136,137],[182]),anchorEnd='מִכָּל חַיֵּי הָעוֹלָם הַבָּא]",')
add(47,'shabbat-youth-that-does-not-return',20,'Shabbat 152a:4',sub(he('Shabbat 152a',4),end='תנא משמיה'),
'It was taught: Rabbi Yosei bar Kisma says: Two [legs] are better than three [two legs and a staff]. Woe for the one thing that goes away and does not return! What is it? Rav Chisda said: Youth. When Rav Dimi came, he said: Youth is a crown of roses; old age is a crown of thorns.',
'The saying laments the physical powers of youth that cannot be recovered. The ois sets this physical decline beside the different trajectory of wisdom acquired through Torah.',pg([137],[182]))
add(47,'kinnim-elders-of-torah',21,'Mishnah Kinnim 3:6',sub(he('Mishnah Kinnim 3:6'),'רבי שמעון בן עקשיא'),
'Rabbi Shimon ben Akashya says: As the elders of the unlearned grow older, their minds become more confused, as it is stated, “He removes speech from the trusted, and takes away the judgment of elders.” But the elders of Torah are not so: as they grow older, their minds become more settled, as it is stated, “With the aged is wisdom, and with length of days is understanding.”',
'The Mishnah gives the exact contrast cited here: the loss of physical youth need not entail a decline in the wisdom of Torah.',pg([137],[182,183]))
add(47,'shemot-rabbah-honor-of-elders',22,'Shemot Rabbah 5:12',sub(he('Shemot Rabbah 5:12'),end='רבי אבין אמר'),
'“Moshe and Aharon went and gathered,” and so forth. Rabbi Akiva said: Why are Israel compared to a bird? Just as a bird flies only with its wings, so Israel can endure only through the elders. Great is the standing of the elders: if they are old, they are beloved before the Holy One, blessed be He; if they are young, their youth takes a secondary place. Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai taught: In several places we learned that the Holy One, blessed be He, accorded honor to the elders. At the burning bush, it is written, “Go and gather the elders of Israel.” At Sinai, it is written, “To Moshe He said: Ascend to Hashem,” and so forth. At the Tent of Meeting, it is written, “Moshe called Aharon and his sons,” and so forth. In the future as well, it is stated, “For Hashem of hosts will reign on Mount Zion and in Jerusalem, and before His elders there will be honor.”',
'The Midrash presents the elders as indispensable to Israel’s endurance and traces their honor through successive revelations. Its reference to young elders shows that this standing is not simply a measure of physical age.',pg([138],[183]),anchorEnd='מֵחֲשִׁיבוּת הַזְּקֵנִים,')
add(48,'mekhilta-association-even-for-torah',1,'Mekhilta DeRabbi Yishmael, Tractate Amalek 3:10',sub(he('Mekhilta DeRabbi Yishmael, Tractate Amalek 3',10),'שנאמר'),
'As it is stated, “Because you associated with Achazyahu, Hashem has shattered your works.” From here the Sages said: A person should not associate with a wicked person, even in order to bring him close to Torah.',
'This supplies the Mekhilta’s actual inference, beyond the biblical verse already quoted in the existing note. It is the strong formulation that Rav Tzadok is explaining at the opening of this ois.',pg([137,138],[183]),anchorEnd='בַּמְּכִילְתָּא,')
add(48,'bereshit-rabbah-potiphars-wife-and-daughter',8,'Bereshit Rabbah 85:2',sub(he('Bereshit Rabbah 85:2'),'רבי שמואל בר נחמן אמר','ודכותה'),
'Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachman said: [The passages are adjacent] to place Tamar’s act beside the act of Potiphar’s wife. Just as this one acted for the sake of Heaven, so did that one. For Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi said: She saw in her astrology that she was destined to have a son from him, but she did not know whether it would be from herself or from her daughter. Thus it is written, “Those who announce by the months some of what will come upon you.” Rabbi Aivu said: “Some of what”—not all of what.',
'The Midrash speaks of Potiphar’s wife and her incomplete knowledge. Rav Tzadok develops the implications for Yosef; the original passage itself attributes the astrological vision to her.',pg([139,140],[184,185]))
add(48,'yisrael-kedoshim-asenath-and-upbringing',7,'Yisrael Kedoshim 10:15',sub(he('Yisrael Kedoshim 10:15'),'ובמדרש אמרו','ולפי שהיא'),
'The Midrash says that she saw that he would marry her daughter, for Potiphar is Poti-phera. Elsewhere it is said that Asenath was Dinah’s daughter from Shechem. Certainly both are true, except that one who raises a child is regarded as having given birth to the child. They raised her, and therefore she is called their daughter; presumably they truly have a connection to her like that of a parent who gave birth. Thus Moshe our teacher, peace be upon him, is called the son of Bityah.',
'Rav Tzadok reconciles the two accounts of Asenath’s parentage through the significance of upbringing. He retains a real connection between the child and the people who raised her, which helps explain the reference to “her daughter.”',pg([139],[185]),anchorEnd='בִּתָּהּ',url='#/read/Yisrael_Kedoshim_10',readerRef='Yisrael Kedoshim 10:15')
E[-1]['sourceEvidence'][0]['url']='https://www.sefaria.org/Yisrael_Kedoshim_10:15'
add(48,'maharal-gehinnom-as-privation',6,'Gevurot Hashem 34:3',he('Gevurot Hashem 34:3'),
'There is also absence in the world. This is because deficiency adheres to created existence: existence is not absolutely complete, but absence adheres to the creation of the world. This is Gehinnom, which is the absence of created existence. Therefore, whoever is suited to destruction and absence comes to Gehinnom, where there is absence and deficiency of created existence. When the Holy One, blessed be He, brought the plague of darkness upon Egypt, He brought absence upon them, because absence and deficiency adhere to creation.',
'Maharal uses Gehinnom to describe privation—the loss or absence of created being. This clarifies the terminology Rav Tzadok uses when contrasting loss with the positive good of the World to Come.',pg([138,139],[184]))
add(49,'shevet-musar-where-the-inclination-resists',0,'Shevet Musar 1:4',sub(he('Shevet Musar 1',4),'וכשם שבדבר'),
'Just as, from the subject a person most desires to study, he can understand that he has been reincarnated to complete that matter, so a person must know the root of his soul, from where it is drawn, and what he has come to repair and complete, as the Zohar on Song of Songs says concerning “Tell me, you whom my soul loves,” and so forth. To understand this, he should see which mitzvah his inclination most forcefully urges him to neglect, and strengthen himself to fulfill it. Certainly he has been reincarnated for that mitzvah. To prevent him from completing his task, his inclination opposes him and urges him to neglect it, so as to take him from the world empty-handed. Thus you find among the accomplished sages of the Talmud that, through their great wisdom, they perceived which mitzvah they had been reincarnated to fulfill, and held firmly to it more than to the other mitzvot. As it is said in Shabbat 118b, Rav Yosef asked Rav Yosef son of Rabba: In which mitzvah was your father especially careful? He answered: In the mitzvah of tzitzit, and so forth. Others were careful in other mitzvot, because through their wisdom they recognized what they had come to complete.',
'The source explicitly connects unusually strong resistance to a particular task of spiritual repair. Its parallel with a person’s preferred area of Torah study supplies the two ways of discerning that task.',pg([140,141],[185,186]))
E[-1]['reviewEvidence']['locatorNote']='Printed chapter1 section13 is contained in canonical Sefaria1:4.'
add(49,'berakhot-different-faces-and-minds',5,'Berakhot 58a:3',sub(he('Berakhot 58a',3),end='בן זומא'),
'The Sages taught: One who sees a multitude of Israel says: Blessed is the Knower of secrets, for their minds are not alike and their faces are not alike.',
'The Gemara pairs differences of understanding with differences of appearance. Rav Tzadok uses this as a basis for the distinct task belonging to each soul.',pg([142],[187]),anchorEnd='פַּרְצוּפִים שָׁוִים,')
add(49,'etz-chaim-special-mitzvah-emphasis',6,'Sefer Etz Chaim 49:5:1',sub(he('Sefer Etz Chaim 49:5',1),'לכן לפי ניצוץ נשמת האדם'),
'Therefore, according to the spark of a person’s soul, he is especially bound to fulfill the mitzvot of that part. For one who belongs to the aspect from the loins upward does not have the mitzvah of removing forbidden fat [as his particular task of rectification], since he does not need it. But if he eats it, he will certainly cause damage; nevertheless, he will not initially be reincarnated for this. Therefore the early masters, who knew where their spark depended, attached themselves to certain mitzvot more than to others. One who understands will understand. [Gloss—Tzemach: This is the meaning of their question: In what was your father especially careful?]',
'This passage discusses a soul’s particular work of rectification and the reason for reincarnation. It expressly retains the damage caused by transgression even when that mitzvah is not the soul’s primary task; the distinction does not exempt anyone from observing the mitzvah.',pg([142,143],[187,188]))
add(50,'sanhedrin-redemption-in-its-time',0,'Sanhedrin 98a:12',he('Sanhedrin 98a',12),
'Rabbi Alexandri said: Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi raised a contradiction. It is written, “In its time,” and it is written, “I shall hasten it.” If they merit it, “I shall hasten it”; if they do not merit it, “In its time.”',
'The Gemara distinguishes the appointed time from an earlier arrival brought by merit. This supplies the source for the ois’s discussion of “in its time, I shall hasten it.”',pg([142,143],[188]))
add(50,'megillah-remedy-before-blow',3,'Megillah 13b:12-13',he('Megillah 13b',12)+' '+he('Megillah 13b',13),
'“After these events”—after what? Rava said: After the Holy One, blessed be He, had created a remedy for the blow. For Reish Lakish said: The Holy One, blessed be He, does not strike Israel unless He first creates a remedy for them, as it is stated, “When I would heal Israel, the sin of Ephraim is revealed.” But it is not so with the nations of the world: He strikes them and afterward creates a remedy for them, as it is stated, “Hashem will strike Egypt, striking and healing.”',
'The saying is introduced through the order of events in Esther. Both the rule about Israel and the contrasting order for the nations are retained, so the quoted principle keeps its original scope.',pg([143,144],[188,189]))
add(50,'rashbam-consoling-rav-ilish',4,'Rashbam on Bava Batra 133b:3:2',he('Rashbam on Bava Batra 133b',3,2),
'“He applied the verse to him”: Rava did so to console him: “I am Hashem; in its time I shall hasten it.” At the time when the righteous need salvation, the Holy One, blessed be He, provides it for them. So too with you: I know that no erroneous judgment has ever come about through you, for now the Holy One, blessed be He, arranged for me to be here before you reached a ruling, and you did not stumble.',
'Rashbam specifies that Rava arrived before Rav Ilish issued the ruling. This is the distinction the ois draws between beginning to make a mistake and allowing an erroneous judgment to take effect.',pg([144],[189]))
