add(46,'likkutei-maamarim-kingdom-without-crown',13,'Likkutei Maamarim, On Book of Joshua 28',local('likkutei-maamarim','Likkutei Maamarim, On Book of Joshua 28',6,14),
'Boldness means audacity: kingship without a crown, even though it is effective even toward Heaven. “Toward” means “against” in the holy tongue—that is, opposition; one who opposes the will of the blessed God can also accomplish something. Therefore Yosef too attained kingship through the intensity of his desire for it, as his brothers said to him, “Will you indeed reign over us?” His dreams likewise arose from his thoughts during the day. He attained it by the strength of his righteousness, and this is “kingship without a crown.” “Crown” means the beginning and hidden root of every complete structure of ten sefirot, as is known. It means an extension of kingship that has no root in its upper source, from whose power comes enduring existence. It exists only in the extension of the nine attributes revealed in this world, through the force of his righteousness, good deeds, and intense desire for it.',
'Rav Tzadok explains the metaphor directly: the crown is the hidden root that gives rule enduring existence. This distinguishes power won by intense personal striving from power rooted in the divine will.',pg([131,132],[177]),url='#/read/Likkutei_Maamarim,_On_Book_of_Joshua',readerRef='Likkutei Maamarim, On Book of Joshua 28')
add(47,'divrei-soferim-character-for-heaven',2,'Divrei Soferim 28:1',local('divrei-soferim','Divrei Soferim 28:1',5,8),
'But in perfecting one’s character, even when the traits are naturally implanted within him, he should not employ them merely because of his nature, but for the sake of Heaven, to act according to the will of the blessed God. When he acts harshly where God does not desire compassion, he is the truly compassionate person, for this is compassion toward truth: acting harshly toward them is “a benefit to them and a benefit to the world.” Through “I shall cut off the horns of the wicked,” “the horns of the righteous shall be raised,” and all abundance and blessing increase in the world through this.',
'This parallel states the ois’s governing principle in direct terms: a natural trait gains its proper value through the purpose for which it is used.',pg([133,134],[178,179]),url='#/read/Divrei_Soferim_28',readerRef='Divrei Soferim 28:1')
add(47,'divrei-soferim-seventy-powers',16,'Divrei Soferim 29:1',local('divrei-soferim','Divrei Soferim 29:1',0,2),
'The totality of good character traits consists of seven, each containing ten. Corresponding to them are the seventy souls who descended to Egypt, each one a distinct power in holiness. Opposite them are seventy nations, each one a distinct power in the realm of impurity, expressed in a particular evil trait; their roots are the seven nations of the Land of Israel.',
'Rav Tzadok supplies the numerical structure behind the phrase “seventy nations and seventy powers of the soul”: seven root traits, each comprising ten aspects.',pg([136],[181,182]),url='#/read/Divrei_Soferim_29',readerRef='Divrei Soferim 29:1')
